---
title: One-Shot Extraction
id: structured-outputs-one-shot
order: 2
description: "Extract a single typed object from a prompt with chat({ outputSchema }). Field descriptions, nested schemas, plain JSON Schema, error handling — everything you need for a single round-trip from text in to typed data out."
keywords:
  - tanstack ai
  - structured outputs
  - one-shot extraction
  - chat outputSchema
  - zod
  - json schema
  - type inference
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You have unstructured input — a paragraph of text, a freeform user prompt, the body of an email — and you want exactly one typed object back. No streaming, no history, no agent loop: one prompt in, one validated object out."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By the end of this guide you'll have a working "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ outputSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":" call returning a fully-typed result, know how to describe fields so the model fills them correctly, and have a pattern for handling validation errors."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" If you want to stream the result field-by-field into a UI, you want "},{"type":"link","href":"./streaming","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming UIs"}]},{"type":"text","value":" instead. If you want users to iterate on the object across multiple turns, you want "},{"type":"link","href":"./multi-turn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-Turn Chat"}]},{"type":"text","value":". If the model must inspect files in a sandbox first, you want "},{"type":"link","href":"./harnesses","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Harness Agents"}]},{"type":"text","value":". This page is for the single-extraction case."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"basic-usage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic Usage"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Define a schema and pass it as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":". The return type follows from the schema — no cast needed."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string().meta({ description: \"The person's full name\" }),\n  age: z.number().meta({ description: \"The person's age in years\" }),\n  email: z.string().email().meta({ description: \"The person's email address\" }),\n});\n\nconst person = await chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [\n    {\n      role: \"user\",\n      content:\n        \"Extract the person info: John Doe is 30 years old, email john@example.com\",\n    },\n  ],\n  outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n});\n\nperson.name;  // string\nperson.age;   // number\nperson.email; // string","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-inference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Inference"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The return type of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" switches on the combination of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Return type"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream: false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Promise<ChatResult>"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ text, chunks }"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream: true"},{"type":"text","value":" (default for plain chat)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" (this page — implicitly non-streaming)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Promise<InferSchemaType<TSchema>>"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream: true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StructuredOutputStream<InferSchemaType<TSchema>>"},{"type":"text","value":" (see "},{"type":"link","href":"./streaming","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming UIs"}]},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The TypeScript type of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"person"},{"type":"text","value":" above is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ name: string; age: number; email: string }"},{"type":"text","value":" — derived from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PersonSchema"},{"type":"text","value":". No runtime cast, no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"as"},{"type":"text","value":", no separate type definition."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"field-descriptions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Field Descriptions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Field descriptions tell the model what data to extract. They become part of the JSON Schema sent to the provider — the model sees them as hints. In Zod v4.2+ use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".meta()"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst ProductSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string().meta({ description: \"The product name\" }),\n  price: z.number().meta({ description: \"Price in USD\" }),\n  inStock: z.boolean().meta({\n    description: \"Whether the product is currently available\",\n  }),\n  categories: z\n    .array(z.string())\n    .meta({\n      description:\n        \"Product categories like 'electronics', 'clothing', etc.\",\n    }),\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Descriptions earn their keep when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The field name is ambiguous ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"price"},{"type":"text","value":" — in what currency?)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The expected unit isn't obvious ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"duration"},{"type":"text","value":" — seconds or minutes?)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The schema is being applied to text where the same concept could be phrased many ways"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"complex-nested-schemas","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complex Nested Schemas"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schemas can nest arbitrarily. The inferred type follows the structure."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { anthropicText } from \"@tanstack/ai-anthropic\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst CompanySchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  founded: z.number().meta({ description: \"Year the company was founded\" }),\n  headquarters: z.object({\n    city: z.string(),\n    country: z.string(),\n    address: z.string().optional(),\n  }),\n  employees: z.array(\n    z.object({\n      name: z.string(),\n      role: z.string(),\n      department: z.string(),\n    }),\n  ),\n  financials: z\n    .object({\n      revenue: z.number().meta({ description: \"Annual revenue in millions USD\" }),\n      profitable: z.boolean(),\n    })\n    .optional(),\n});\n\nconst company = await chat({\n  adapter: anthropicText(\"claude-sonnet-4-6\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Extract company info from this article: ...\" }],\n  outputSchema: CompanySchema,\n});\n\ncompany.headquarters.city; // string\ncompany.employees[0]!.role; // string\ncompany.financials?.profitable; // boolean | undefined","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"using-plain-json-schema","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using Plain JSON Schema"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you don't want a schema library, pass a JSON Schema object directly. The trade-off: TypeScript can't infer the return type, so the result is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":" and you take responsibility for the runtime shape."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport type { JSONSchema } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nconst schema: JSONSchema = {\n  type: \"object\",\n  properties: {\n    name: { type: \"string\", description: \"The person's name\" },\n    age: { type: \"number\", description: \"The person's age\" },\n  },\n  required: [\"name\", \"age\"],\n};\n\nconst result = await chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Extract: John is 25 years old\" }],\n  outputSchema: schema,\n});\n\n// `result` is `unknown` — a raw JSON Schema gives no compile-time type.\n// Validate it (e.g. with a Standard Schema library) before use.","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer a schema library when you can — type inference is worth it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"error-handling","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error Handling"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the model's response doesn't satisfy your schema, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" throws a validation error. The message includes the failing fields."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { MySchema } from \"./schemas\";\n\ntry {\n  const result = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"...\" }],\n    outputSchema: MySchema,\n  });\n} catch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof Error) {\n    console.error(\"Structured output failed:\", error.message);\n    // The message names which fields failed validation.\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provider-level errors (auth failure, rate limit, network) throw the same way — wrap the call in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"try"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"catch"},{"type":"text","value":" to handle both."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"read-the-provider-s-error","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read the provider's error"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When the provider rejects the request, the message can be short, for example "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Provider returned error"},{"type":"text","value":". The provider's full error body is on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error.cause"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { MySchema } from \"./schemas\";\n\ntry {\n  const result = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-6-astra\"),\n    messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"...\" }],\n    outputSchema: MySchema,\n  });\n} catch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof Error) {\n    console.error(error.message, error.cause);\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"recover-the-model-s-text","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recover the model's text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sometimes a model returns a valid JSON object and then more text, for example a second copy of the object. The JSON parse then fails and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" throws. The error message shows only the first 200 characters. The full text is on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error.rawText"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { MySchema } from \"./schemas\";\n\ntry {\n  const result = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-6-astra\"),\n    messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"...\" }],\n    outputSchema: MySchema,\n  });\n} catch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof Error && \"rawText\" in error) {\n    console.error(\"Model text:\", error.rawText);\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parse and validate that text yourself if you want to keep the answer."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"consuming-the-result-on-the-client","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consuming the result on the client"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"await chat({ outputSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":" call above returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Promise<T>"},{"type":"text","value":" — ideal for a server route, a script, or a CLI. There are two ways that typed object reaches a browser."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"as-plain-json-no-hook","children":[{"type":"text","value":"As plain JSON (no hook)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the client only needs the finished object and you don't want progressive UI, resolve the promise on the server and return it as JSON. The browser fetches it like any other endpoint — no TanStack client API, no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\n// server route\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { text } = await request.json();\n  const person = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: `Extract the person info: ${text}` }],\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  });\n  return Response.json(person); // typed object → JSON\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"code","value":"import { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\n// client\nconst text = \"John Doe, 30, john@example.com\";\nconst res = await fetch(\"/api/extract-person\", {\n  method: \"POST\",\n  body: JSON.stringify({ text }),\n});\nconst person = PersonSchema.parse(await res.json()); // validated + typed","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the most literal one-shot shape: one request, one object back. You own the fetch and the typing; the hook isn't involved."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"with-usechat-typed-final-and-optional-partial","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" — typed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" (and optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you want the hook ergonomics — managed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isLoading"},{"type":"text","value":" state, a schema-typed result, optional field-by-field fill — read "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" off "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat({ outputSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":". Because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" consumes a stream, the server switches to the streaming shape ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream: true"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toServerSentEventsResponse"},{"type":"text","value":"); the client still treats it as \"one object, when it's ready\":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { PersonCard } from \"./PersonCard\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\nfunction PersonExtractor() {\n  // `final` is `z.infer<typeof PersonSchema> | null`.\n  // `partial` is `DeepPartial<z.infer<typeof PersonSchema>>`.\n  const { sendMessage, isLoading, final, partial } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/extract-person\"),\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <button\n        disabled={isLoading}\n        onClick={() => sendMessage(\"Extract: John Doe, 30, john@example.com\")}\n      >\n        Extract\n      </button>\n\n      {/* One-shot UI: just render the validated object when it lands. */}\n      {final && <PersonCard person={final} />}\n    </div>\n  );\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"T | null"},{"type":"text","value":". The validated terminal object, populated when the run completes. For a one-shot UI, render off "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" and you're done."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DeepPartial<T>"},{"type":"text","value":". The same object filling in field by field as the JSON streams. Ignore it if you only care about the finished result; reach for it when you want a progressive form. The "},{"type":"link","href":"./streaming","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming UIs"}]},{"type":"text","value":" guide covers that pattern in depth."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The schema on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is for client-side TypeScript inference (and progressive parsing of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":"). Validation still runs on the server against the schema you pass to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ outputSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For non-streaming adapters (Anthropic, Gemini, Ollama), the object arrives as a single event — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{}"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" snaps in one step. The consumer code above is identical regardless of adapter."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Want the result to fill in field-by-field, or to keep a history of objects across turns? Those are the "},{"type":"link","href":"./streaming","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming UIs"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"link","href":"./multi-turn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-Turn Chat"}]},{"type":"text","value":" journeys — both build on this same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat({ outputSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":" surface."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"best-practices","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best Practices"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"loose":true,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use descriptive field names and descriptions."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The model uses them as hints."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep schemas focused."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Extract only what you need — smaller schemas produce more reliable results."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mark fields optional when they really are."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Don't force the model to invent a value just because the schema demands one."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use enums for constrained values."}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst schema = z.object({\n  status: z.enum([\"pending\", \"approved\", \"rejected\"]),\n  priority: z.enum([\"low\", \"medium\", \"high\"]),\n});","lang":"typescript"}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Test edge cases."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Empty inputs, ambiguous inputs, inputs with extra fields — make sure the schema handles them the way you expect."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"basic-usage","text":"Basic Usage","level":2},{"id":"type-inference","text":"Type Inference","level":2},{"id":"field-descriptions","text":"Field Descriptions","level":2},{"id":"complex-nested-schemas","text":"Complex Nested Schemas","level":2},{"id":"using-plain-json-schema","text":"Using Plain JSON Schema","level":2},{"id":"error-handling","text":"Error Handling","level":2},{"id":"read-the-provider-s-error","text":"Read the provider's error","level":3},{"id":"recover-the-model-s-text","text":"Recover the model's text","level":3},{"id":"consuming-the-result-on-the-client","text":"Consuming the result on the client","level":2},{"id":"as-plain-json-no-hook","text":"As plain JSON (no hook)","level":3},{"id":"with-usechat-typed-final-and-optional-partial","text":"With useChat — typed final (and optional partial)","level":3},{"id":"best-practices","text":"Best Practices","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYou have unstructured input — a paragraph of text, a freeform user prompt, the body of an email — and you want exactly one typed object back. No streaming, no history, no agent loop: one prompt in, one validated object out.\n\nBy the end of this guide you'll have a working `chat({ outputSchema })` call returning a fully-typed result, know how to describe fields so the model fills them correctly, and have a pattern for handling validation errors.\n\n> **Note:** If you want to stream the result field-by-field into a UI, you want [Streaming UIs](./streaming) instead. If you want users to iterate on the object across multiple turns, you want [Multi-Turn Chat](./multi-turn). If the model must inspect files in a sandbox first, you want [Harness Agents](./harnesses). This page is for the single-extraction case.\n\n## Basic Usage\n\nDefine a schema and pass it as `outputSchema`. The return type follows from the schema — no cast needed.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string().meta({ description: \"The person's full name\" }),\n  age: z.number().meta({ description: \"The person's age in years\" }),\n  email: z.string().email().meta({ description: \"The person's email address\" }),\n});\n\nconst person = await chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [\n    {\n      role: \"user\",\n      content:\n        \"Extract the person info: John Doe is 30 years old, email john@example.com\",\n    },\n  ],\n  outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n});\n\nperson.name;  // string\nperson.age;   // number\nperson.email; // string\n```\n\n## Type Inference\n\nThe return type of `chat()` switches on the combination of `outputSchema` and `stream`:\n\n| Configuration | Return type |\n|---|---|\n| No `outputSchema`, `stream: false` | `Promise<ChatResult>` (`{ text, chunks }`) |\n| No `outputSchema`, `stream: true` (default for plain chat) | `AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>` |\n| With `outputSchema` (this page — implicitly non-streaming) | `Promise<InferSchemaType<TSchema>>` |\n| With `outputSchema` and `stream: true` | `StructuredOutputStream<InferSchemaType<TSchema>>` (see [Streaming UIs](./streaming)) |\n\nThe TypeScript type of `person` above is `{ name: string; age: number; email: string }` — derived from `PersonSchema`. No runtime cast, no `as`, no separate type definition.\n\n## Field Descriptions\n\nField descriptions tell the model what data to extract. They become part of the JSON Schema sent to the provider — the model sees them as hints. In Zod v4.2+ use `.meta()`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst ProductSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string().meta({ description: \"The product name\" }),\n  price: z.number().meta({ description: \"Price in USD\" }),\n  inStock: z.boolean().meta({\n    description: \"Whether the product is currently available\",\n  }),\n  categories: z\n    .array(z.string())\n    .meta({\n      description:\n        \"Product categories like 'electronics', 'clothing', etc.\",\n    }),\n});\n```\n\nDescriptions earn their keep when:\n- The field name is ambiguous (`price` — in what currency?)\n- The expected unit isn't obvious (`duration` — seconds or minutes?)\n- The schema is being applied to text where the same concept could be phrased many ways\n\n## Complex Nested Schemas\n\nSchemas can nest arbitrarily. The inferred type follows the structure.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { anthropicText } from \"@tanstack/ai-anthropic\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst CompanySchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  founded: z.number().meta({ description: \"Year the company was founded\" }),\n  headquarters: z.object({\n    city: z.string(),\n    country: z.string(),\n    address: z.string().optional(),\n  }),\n  employees: z.array(\n    z.object({\n      name: z.string(),\n      role: z.string(),\n      department: z.string(),\n    }),\n  ),\n  financials: z\n    .object({\n      revenue: z.number().meta({ description: \"Annual revenue in millions USD\" }),\n      profitable: z.boolean(),\n    })\n    .optional(),\n});\n\nconst company = await chat({\n  adapter: anthropicText(\"claude-sonnet-4-6\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Extract company info from this article: ...\" }],\n  outputSchema: CompanySchema,\n});\n\ncompany.headquarters.city; // string\ncompany.employees[0]!.role; // string\ncompany.financials?.profitable; // boolean | undefined\n```\n\n## Using Plain JSON Schema\n\nIf you don't want a schema library, pass a JSON Schema object directly. The trade-off: TypeScript can't infer the return type, so the result is `unknown` and you take responsibility for the runtime shape.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport type { JSONSchema } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nconst schema: JSONSchema = {\n  type: \"object\",\n  properties: {\n    name: { type: \"string\", description: \"The person's name\" },\n    age: { type: \"number\", description: \"The person's age\" },\n  },\n  required: [\"name\", \"age\"],\n};\n\nconst result = await chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Extract: John is 25 years old\" }],\n  outputSchema: schema,\n});\n\n// `result` is `unknown` — a raw JSON Schema gives no compile-time type.\n// Validate it (e.g. with a Standard Schema library) before use.\n```\n\nPrefer a schema library when you can — type inference is worth it.\n\n## Error Handling\n\nIf the model's response doesn't satisfy your schema, `chat()` throws a validation error. The message includes the failing fields.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { MySchema } from \"./schemas\";\n\ntry {\n  const result = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"...\" }],\n    outputSchema: MySchema,\n  });\n} catch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof Error) {\n    console.error(\"Structured output failed:\", error.message);\n    // The message names which fields failed validation.\n  }\n}\n```\n\nProvider-level errors (auth failure, rate limit, network) throw the same way — wrap the call in `try` / `catch` to handle both.\n\n### Read the provider's error\n\nWhen the provider rejects the request, the message can be short, for example `Provider returned error`. The provider's full error body is on `error.cause`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { MySchema } from \"./schemas\";\n\ntry {\n  const result = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-6-astra\"),\n    messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"...\" }],\n    outputSchema: MySchema,\n  });\n} catch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof Error) {\n    console.error(error.message, error.cause);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Recover the model's text\n\nSometimes a model returns a valid JSON object and then more text, for example a second copy of the object. The JSON parse then fails and `chat()` throws. The error message shows only the first 200 characters. The full text is on `error.rawText`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { MySchema } from \"./schemas\";\n\ntry {\n  const result = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-6-astra\"),\n    messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"...\" }],\n    outputSchema: MySchema,\n  });\n} catch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof Error && \"rawText\" in error) {\n    console.error(\"Model text:\", error.rawText);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nParse and validate that text yourself if you want to keep the answer.\n\n## Consuming the result on the client\n\nThe `await chat({ outputSchema })` call above returns a `Promise<T>` — ideal for a server route, a script, or a CLI. There are two ways that typed object reaches a browser.\n\n### As plain JSON (no hook)\n\nIf the client only needs the finished object and you don't want progressive UI, resolve the promise on the server and return it as JSON. The browser fetches it like any other endpoint — no TanStack client API, no `partial` / `final`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\n// server route\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { text } = await request.json();\n  const person = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: `Extract the person info: ${text}` }],\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  });\n  return Response.json(person); // typed object → JSON\n}\n```\n\n```typescript\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\n// client\nconst text = \"John Doe, 30, john@example.com\";\nconst res = await fetch(\"/api/extract-person\", {\n  method: \"POST\",\n  body: JSON.stringify({ text }),\n});\nconst person = PersonSchema.parse(await res.json()); // validated + typed\n```\n\nThis is the most literal one-shot shape: one request, one object back. You own the fetch and the typing; the hook isn't involved.\n\n### With `useChat` — typed `final` (and optional `partial`)\n\nWhen you want the hook ergonomics — managed `isLoading` state, a schema-typed result, optional field-by-field fill — read `final` off `useChat({ outputSchema })`. Because `useChat` consumes a stream, the server switches to the streaming shape (`stream: true` + `toServerSentEventsResponse`); the client still treats it as \"one object, when it's ready\":\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { PersonCard } from \"./PersonCard\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\nfunction PersonExtractor() {\n  // `final` is `z.infer<typeof PersonSchema> | null`.\n  // `partial` is `DeepPartial<z.infer<typeof PersonSchema>>`.\n  const { sendMessage, isLoading, final, partial } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/extract-person\"),\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <button\n        disabled={isLoading}\n        onClick={() => sendMessage(\"Extract: John Doe, 30, john@example.com\")}\n      >\n        Extract\n      </button>\n\n      {/* One-shot UI: just render the validated object when it lands. */}\n      {final && <PersonCard person={final} />}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n- **`final`** — `T | null`. The validated terminal object, populated when the run completes. For a one-shot UI, render off `final` and you're done.\n- **`partial`** — `DeepPartial<T>`. The same object filling in field by field as the JSON streams. Ignore it if you only care about the finished result; reach for it when you want a progressive form. The [Streaming UIs](./streaming) guide covers that pattern in depth.\n- **The schema on `useChat`** is for client-side TypeScript inference (and progressive parsing of `partial`). Validation still runs on the server against the schema you pass to `chat({ outputSchema })`.\n\nFor non-streaming adapters (Anthropic, Gemini, Ollama), the object arrives as a single event — `partial` stays `{}` and `final` snaps in one step. The consumer code above is identical regardless of adapter.\n\n> Want the result to fill in field-by-field, or to keep a history of objects across turns? Those are the [Streaming UIs](./streaming) and [Multi-Turn Chat](./multi-turn) journeys — both build on this same `useChat({ outputSchema })` surface.\n\n## Best Practices\n\n1. **Use descriptive field names and descriptions.** The model uses them as hints.\n2. **Keep schemas focused.** Extract only what you need — smaller schemas produce more reliable results.\n3. **Mark fields optional when they really are.** Don't force the model to invent a value just because the schema demands one.\n4. **Use enums for constrained values.**\n\n   ```typescript\n   import { z } from \"zod\";\n\n   const schema = z.object({\n     status: z.enum([\"pending\", \"approved\", \"rejected\"]),\n     priority: z.enum([\"low\", \"medium\", \"high\"]),\n   });\n   ```\n\n5. **Test edge cases.** Empty inputs, ambiguous inputs, inputs with extra fields — make sure the schema handles them the way you expect.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: One-Shot Extraction\nid: structured-outputs-one-shot\norder: 2\ndescription: \"Extract a single typed object from a prompt with chat({ outputSchema }). Field descriptions, nested schemas, plain JSON Schema, error handling — everything you need for a single round-trip from text in to typed data out.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - structured outputs\n  - one-shot extraction\n  - chat outputSchema\n  - zod\n  - json schema\n  - type inference\n---\n\nYou have unstructured input — a paragraph of text, a freeform user prompt, the body of an email — and you want exactly one typed object back. No streaming, no history, no agent loop: one prompt in, one validated object out.\n\nBy the end of this guide you'll have a working `chat({ outputSchema })` call returning a fully-typed result, know how to describe fields so the model fills them correctly, and have a pattern for handling validation errors.\n\n> **Note:** If you want to stream the result field-by-field into a UI, you want [Streaming UIs](./streaming) instead. If you want users to iterate on the object across multiple turns, you want [Multi-Turn Chat](./multi-turn). If the model must inspect files in a sandbox first, you want [Harness Agents](./harnesses). This page is for the single-extraction case.\n\n## Basic Usage\n\nDefine a schema and pass it as `outputSchema`. The return type follows from the schema — no cast needed.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string().meta({ description: \"The person's full name\" }),\n  age: z.number().meta({ description: \"The person's age in years\" }),\n  email: z.string().email().meta({ description: \"The person's email address\" }),\n});\n\nconst person = await chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [\n    {\n      role: \"user\",\n      content:\n        \"Extract the person info: John Doe is 30 years old, email john@example.com\",\n    },\n  ],\n  outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n});\n\nperson.name;  // string\nperson.age;   // number\nperson.email; // string\n```\n\n## Type Inference\n\nThe return type of `chat()` switches on the combination of `outputSchema` and `stream`:\n\n| Configuration | Return type |\n|---|---|\n| No `outputSchema`, `stream: false` | `Promise<ChatResult>` (`{ text, chunks }`) |\n| No `outputSchema`, `stream: true` (default for plain chat) | `AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>` |\n| With `outputSchema` (this page — implicitly non-streaming) | `Promise<InferSchemaType<TSchema>>` |\n| With `outputSchema` and `stream: true` | `StructuredOutputStream<InferSchemaType<TSchema>>` (see [Streaming UIs](./streaming)) |\n\nThe TypeScript type of `person` above is `{ name: string; age: number; email: string }` — derived from `PersonSchema`. No runtime cast, no `as`, no separate type definition.\n\n## Field Descriptions\n\nField descriptions tell the model what data to extract. They become part of the JSON Schema sent to the provider — the model sees them as hints. In Zod v4.2+ use `.meta()`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst ProductSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string().meta({ description: \"The product name\" }),\n  price: z.number().meta({ description: \"Price in USD\" }),\n  inStock: z.boolean().meta({\n    description: \"Whether the product is currently available\",\n  }),\n  categories: z\n    .array(z.string())\n    .meta({\n      description:\n        \"Product categories like 'electronics', 'clothing', etc.\",\n    }),\n});\n```\n\nDescriptions earn their keep when:\n- The field name is ambiguous (`price` — in what currency?)\n- The expected unit isn't obvious (`duration` — seconds or minutes?)\n- The schema is being applied to text where the same concept could be phrased many ways\n\n## Complex Nested Schemas\n\nSchemas can nest arbitrarily. The inferred type follows the structure.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { anthropicText } from \"@tanstack/ai-anthropic\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst CompanySchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  founded: z.number().meta({ description: \"Year the company was founded\" }),\n  headquarters: z.object({\n    city: z.string(),\n    country: z.string(),\n    address: z.string().optional(),\n  }),\n  employees: z.array(\n    z.object({\n      name: z.string(),\n      role: z.string(),\n      department: z.string(),\n    }),\n  ),\n  financials: z\n    .object({\n      revenue: z.number().meta({ description: \"Annual revenue in millions USD\" }),\n      profitable: z.boolean(),\n    })\n    .optional(),\n});\n\nconst company = await chat({\n  adapter: anthropicText(\"claude-sonnet-4-6\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Extract company info from this article: ...\" }],\n  outputSchema: CompanySchema,\n});\n\ncompany.headquarters.city; // string\ncompany.employees[0]!.role; // string\ncompany.financials?.profitable; // boolean | undefined\n```\n\n## Using Plain JSON Schema\n\nIf you don't want a schema library, pass a JSON Schema object directly. The trade-off: TypeScript can't infer the return type, so the result is `unknown` and you take responsibility for the runtime shape.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport type { JSONSchema } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nconst schema: JSONSchema = {\n  type: \"object\",\n  properties: {\n    name: { type: \"string\", description: \"The person's name\" },\n    age: { type: \"number\", description: \"The person's age\" },\n  },\n  required: [\"name\", \"age\"],\n};\n\nconst result = await chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Extract: John is 25 years old\" }],\n  outputSchema: schema,\n});\n\n// `result` is `unknown` — a raw JSON Schema gives no compile-time type.\n// Validate it (e.g. with a Standard Schema library) before use.\n```\n\nPrefer a schema library when you can — type inference is worth it.\n\n## Error Handling\n\nIf the model's response doesn't satisfy your schema, `chat()` throws a validation error. The message includes the failing fields.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { MySchema } from \"./schemas\";\n\ntry {\n  const result = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"...\" }],\n    outputSchema: MySchema,\n  });\n} catch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof Error) {\n    console.error(\"Structured output failed:\", error.message);\n    // The message names which fields failed validation.\n  }\n}\n```\n\nProvider-level errors (auth failure, rate limit, network) throw the same way — wrap the call in `try` / `catch` to handle both.\n\n### Read the provider's error\n\nWhen the provider rejects the request, the message can be short, for example `Provider returned error`. The provider's full error body is on `error.cause`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { MySchema } from \"./schemas\";\n\ntry {\n  const result = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-6-astra\"),\n    messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"...\" }],\n    outputSchema: MySchema,\n  });\n} catch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof Error) {\n    console.error(error.message, error.cause);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Recover the model's text\n\nSometimes a model returns a valid JSON object and then more text, for example a second copy of the object. The JSON parse then fails and `chat()` throws. The error message shows only the first 200 characters. The full text is on `error.rawText`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { MySchema } from \"./schemas\";\n\ntry {\n  const result = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-6-astra\"),\n    messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"...\" }],\n    outputSchema: MySchema,\n  });\n} catch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof Error && \"rawText\" in error) {\n    console.error(\"Model text:\", error.rawText);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nParse and validate that text yourself if you want to keep the answer.\n\n## Consuming the result on the client\n\nThe `await chat({ outputSchema })` call above returns a `Promise<T>` — ideal for a server route, a script, or a CLI. There are two ways that typed object reaches a browser.\n\n### As plain JSON (no hook)\n\nIf the client only needs the finished object and you don't want progressive UI, resolve the promise on the server and return it as JSON. The browser fetches it like any other endpoint — no TanStack client API, no `partial` / `final`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\n// server route\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { text } = await request.json();\n  const person = await chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: `Extract the person info: ${text}` }],\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  });\n  return Response.json(person); // typed object → JSON\n}\n```\n\n```typescript\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\n// client\nconst text = \"John Doe, 30, john@example.com\";\nconst res = await fetch(\"/api/extract-person\", {\n  method: \"POST\",\n  body: JSON.stringify({ text }),\n});\nconst person = PersonSchema.parse(await res.json()); // validated + typed\n```\n\nThis is the most literal one-shot shape: one request, one object back. You own the fetch and the typing; the hook isn't involved.\n\n### With `useChat` — typed `final` (and optional `partial`)\n\nWhen you want the hook ergonomics — managed `isLoading` state, a schema-typed result, optional field-by-field fill — read `final` off `useChat({ outputSchema })`. Because `useChat` consumes a stream, the server switches to the streaming shape (`stream: true` + `toServerSentEventsResponse`); the client still treats it as \"one object, when it's ready\":\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { PersonCard } from \"./PersonCard\";\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number(),\n  email: z.string().email(),\n});\n\nfunction PersonExtractor() {\n  // `final` is `z.infer<typeof PersonSchema> | null`.\n  // `partial` is `DeepPartial<z.infer<typeof PersonSchema>>`.\n  const { sendMessage, isLoading, final, partial } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/extract-person\"),\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <button\n        disabled={isLoading}\n        onClick={() => sendMessage(\"Extract: John Doe, 30, john@example.com\")}\n      >\n        Extract\n      </button>\n\n      {/* One-shot UI: just render the validated object when it lands. */}\n      {final && <PersonCard person={final} />}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n- **`final`** — `T | null`. The validated terminal object, populated when the run completes. For a one-shot UI, render off `final` and you're done.\n- **`partial`** — `DeepPartial<T>`. The same object filling in field by field as the JSON streams. Ignore it if you only care about the finished result; reach for it when you want a progressive form. The [Streaming UIs](./streaming) guide covers that pattern in depth.\n- **The schema on `useChat`** is for client-side TypeScript inference (and progressive parsing of `partial`). Validation still runs on the server against the schema you pass to `chat({ outputSchema })`.\n\nFor non-streaming adapters (Anthropic, Gemini, Ollama), the object arrives as a single event — `partial` stays `{}` and `final` snaps in one step. The consumer code above is identical regardless of adapter.\n\n> Want the result to fill in field-by-field, or to keep a history of objects across turns? Those are the [Streaming UIs](./streaming) and [Multi-Turn Chat](./multi-turn) journeys — both build on this same `useChat({ outputSchema })` surface.\n\n## Best Practices\n\n1. **Use descriptive field names and descriptions.** The model uses them as hints.\n2. **Keep schemas focused.** Extract only what you need — smaller schemas produce more reliable results.\n3. **Mark fields optional when they really are.** Don't force the model to invent a value just because the schema demands one.\n4. **Use enums for constrained values.**\n\n   ```typescript\n   import { z } from \"zod\";\n\n   const schema = z.object({\n     status: z.enum([\"pending\", \"approved\", \"rejected\"]),\n     priority: z.enum([\"low\", \"medium\", \"high\"]),\n   });\n   ```\n\n5. **Test edge cases.** Empty inputs, ambiguous inputs, inputs with extra fields — make sure the schema handles them the way you expect.\n"}
